I feel an urge to make a few comments on that...

> Consider using HTML with a fixed point font, then
> saving the displayed browser output in text mode.

If I save a webpage with my browser in text mode I don't get a
formatting that is anyhow comparable with the quality of the *roff
family of programs; I've just tried that, and even with a very
simple table it doesn't seem to work well. It seems you will have to
do some postprocessing to get a pretty formatted table. (But if
there's something specific I missed to consider when doing that I'd
be interested to hear about it, Kent.)

> If
> you once learn how to write raw HTML code, you can
> get it to jump through some pretty amazing hoops,
> and it isn't a very high learning curve. Most people
> learn HTML coding simply, to start at least, by
> looking at other people's HTML coding.

Yes, for a web presentation that's fine. (But I read that the OP
wants pretty formatted _text files_.)

> HTML has a very powerful table facility, several
> good forms of lists, good paragraphing skills, and
> more "text box definition" stuff than you'd probably
> ever need to learn to use. It also has a style sheet
> functionality you might find interesting that lets
> the same text be presented (with some extra effort
> to parameterize the markup for styles) several
> alternative ways just by changing the name of an
> associated style sheet.

All very intersting and powerful features for a web presentation,
not so much for text file output. (And even for web presentation one
should mention at least that the output can look quite different on
different browsers.)

> Others here have recommended tools in the fairly
> ancient (1980s) [ngt]roff family, which can produce
> quite pretty output "eventually", but that uses
